" ============================================================================
" Nonlinear Finite Element Simulation of Hyperelastic Deformation
" (Compressible Neo-Hookean Hyperelasticity in deal.II)
" ============================================================================
"
" Problem Overview
" ---------------
" This project implements a nonlinear finite element solver for large-
" deformation elasticity using the compressible Neo-Hookean hyperelastic
" material model. The goal is to compute the equilibrium deformation of an
" elastic body subjected to increasing external loads, capturing geometric and
" material nonlinearities that cannot be represented by linear elasticity.
"
" The solver is written in C++ using deal.II and follows a total Lagrangian
" formulation, making it representative of the numerical methods used in modern
" high-performance simulation engines.
"
"
" Physical Problem Description
" ----------------------------
" We consider a two-dimensional elastic body occupying a rectangular reference
" domain:
"
"   Ω = [0, L] × [0, H].
"
" The unknown field is the displacement vector:
"
"   u(X) ∈ R^2,
"
" which maps material points from the reference configuration to the deformed
" configuration.
"
" Boundary conditions:
"   - Dirichlet (clamped): left edge x = 0 is fixed:
"       u = 0.
"   - Neumann (traction): horizontal traction applied on right edge x = L.
"
" The applied traction is incrementally ramped from zero to its full magnitude
" using load stepping to improve robustness of the nonlinear solve.
"
"
" Governing Equations
" -------------------
" Kinematics:
"   F = I + ∇u
"   J = det(F)
"
" Constitutive model (compressible Neo-Hookean):
"   First Piola–Kirchhoff stress:
"
"     P = μ (F − F^{−T}) + λ ln(J) F^{−T}
"
" where μ and λ are Lamé parameters. The dependence on F, F^{-1}, and ln(J)
" introduces strong nonlinearity.
"
"
" Weak Formulation
" ----------------
" The equilibrium configuration satisfies:
"
"   ∫_Ω  ∇v : P(F(u)) dX  =  ∫_{Γ_N} v · t dS   for all test functions v.
"
"
" Numerical Method
" ----------------
" Spatial discretization:
"   - Vector-valued continuous finite elements (Q1 bilinear by default).
"
" Nonlinear solver:
"   - Newton–Raphson iterations with consistent tangent stiffness.
"
" Continuation / load stepping:
"   - Apply traction scaled by α ∈ [0, 1] over several load steps.
"
" Linear solve per Newton iteration:
"   - Preconditioned Conjugate Gradient (CG) with SSOR preconditioner.
"
" Newton update:
"   - Solve   K(u_k) δu = −R(u_k)
"   - Update  u_{k+1} = u_k + δu
"
"
" Output and Visualization
" ------------------------
" The solver writes displacement results per load step to VTU files suitable for
" ParaView. Typical observables:
"   - Smooth displacement fields
"   - Increasing deformation with load step
"   - Poisson contraction in the transverse direction
"   - Nonlinear response due to large deformation effects
"
"
" Why This Problem Matters
" ------------------------
" This project demonstrates:
"   - Implementation of a nonlinear PDE/FEM solver in modern C++
"   - Residual and Jacobian assembly for vector-valued fields
"   - Robust nonlinear solution strategy (Newton + continuation)
"   - Sparse linear algebra and constraint handling in a large C++ framework
"
" The workflow mirrors industrial simulation engines and is directly relevant to
" roles requiring algorithms, performance awareness, and numerical robustness.
" ============================================================================

